            <ArticleTitle>2017 American College of Rheumatology Guideline for the Prevention and Treatment of Glucocorticoid-Induced Osteoporosis.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1002/acr.23416</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The Voting Panel recommended oral bisphosphonates as a first choice and parenteral bisphosphonates as a second choice after comparing data about absolute fracture reduction, harms (toxicity and inconvenience of daily injections), and costs. Drs. Maricic, Deal, Dore, and Laster cite the randomized trial of alendronate versus teriparatide in glucocorticoid-treated patients that reported incident fractures as a secondary outcome. This trial found a lower risk of clinical vertebral fractures in the teriparatide compared to alendronate treated patients (0% vs. 2.4% [n=4/169], p=0.037) but no difference in the risk of nonvertebral fracture (7.5% vs. 7.0%, p=0.843) (1). This article is protected by copyright. All rights reserved.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>This article is protected by copyright. All rights reserved.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
